# Keeps the build context small. Docker packages this whole directory and ships
# it to the daemon *before* the first instruction runs, so anything listed here
# is never transferred at all. Without this file the context was 732 MB - 612 MB
# of it `.venv` and 65 MB `.fastembed_cache` - paid on every `docker compose up
# --build` and every ACR push.
#
# This changes build time only, never the image: the Dockerfile copies named
# paths (`pyproject.toml`, `poetry.lock`, `app`, `alembic.ini`,
# `docker-entrypoint.sh`) and never `COPY . .`. Contrast `frontend/.dockerignore`,
# where the equivalent entry is load-bearing because that build *does* use
# `COPY . .` and would otherwise overwrite the Linux `node_modules` with the
# host's Windows one.
#
# Deliberately limited to generated and machine-local artefacts. `tests/`,
# `scripts/` and `evals/` are not copied into the image either, but they are real
# source - excluding them here would silently drop them if this Dockerfile ever
# moves to `COPY . .`, which is exactly the trap the frontend comment describes.

# Local virtualenv - Windows-native, and the image builds its own at /app/.venv.
.venv/

# Model weights, downloaded at runtime into a mounted volume, never baked in.
.fastembed_cache/

# Python and tool caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.deepeval/

# Secrets. The image receives configuration as environment variables; a .env
# copied into a layer would outlive the container it was meant for.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Eval run artefacts - per-run scratch, regenerated on demand.
evals/results/
